[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Sicilian Najdorf"]
[Variation "Sicilian Defense: Najdorf Variation, Lipnitsky Attack"]
[ECO "B90"]
[Character "sharp,positional"]
[Risk "medium"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "queenside-expansion,solid-centre"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6
6. Bc4 e6 { Blocks the diagonal of the bishop on c4 with a pawn, so f7 is not a target. }
7. Bb3 (7. O-O Be7 { Black develops while White is still choosing a plan. } 8. Bb3 { This reaches the main line by a different move order. } 8... b5)
7... b5 { Gains space on the queenside and prepares ...Bb7 on the long diagonal. }
8. O-O Be7 { Unpins the knight on f6 and gets ready to castle. }
9. Qf3 (9. Re1 O-O { Black castles first; the king is safe and ...Bb7 can follow. })
9... Qb6 { Hits the knight on d4 and the pawn on b2, so White cannot just keep building up. }
10. Be3 Qb7 { Steps out of the way of Nd5 and Nxe6 ideas. From b7 the queen also protects the b5 pawn and eyes the e4 pawn. }
11. Qg3 Nc6 { Challenges the knight on d4. Trading it removes White's most active piece. }
12. Nxc6 Qxc6 { The queen recaptures and eyes the long diagonal and the c-file. } *
